.TH CSTRING_RFIND 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cstring_rfind \- find the last occurrence of a substring
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>

size_t cstring_rfind(cstr s, const char *needle);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Searches the string for the last occurrence of needle and returns its
zero-based starting index. Occurrences that overlap count, so the last
occurrence of "aba" in "ababa" starts at index 2.

For a string of 256 bytes or more, the search runs from the end of the
string toward its start and stops at the first match that it meets, so
the bytes to the left of that match are never read. Its time is then
linear in the length of the string plus the length of needle, whatever
the two hold. A shorter string is searched from its start, which is
faster at that length.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
s        String to search
needle   Substring to find
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Zero-based index of the last occurrence
cstring_length(s) if needle is an empty string (the past-the-end position)
ccol_invalid_size ((size_t)-1) if needle is not found, or if needle is NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Asserts if s is NULL
A needle of up to 128 bytes needs no memory. A longer needle can make
the search take one temporary table (one size_t per byte of needle) from
the allocator of s, and the search frees that table before the call
returns. When that allocator refuses the table, the search still gives
the same answer, but its time can then grow with the product of the two
lengths
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cstring_find (3)
